<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8280"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Iceteajan<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Iceteajan<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 21, 2005 - 5:27 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Whatthehell, <BR> <BR>The Coleman “statistical oddity” is indeed noteworthy.   <BR> <BR>But there are/were small towns with populations that can &#40;or must&#41; almost exclusively thrive upon a single large corporation.   &#40;Say perhaps a large auto manufacturer.&#41;  So in these type cases, perhaps 1 in 3 people work at such a corporation.   <BR> <BR>I don’t know if Coleman falls into this type of circumstance, but I would welcome any number crunchers that would reasonably bear out the statistics.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
