<!--Post: 8292--><!--Time: 1109039701-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8292"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Iceteajan<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Iceteajan<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 21, 2005 - 6:35 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Does anyone know if the &#34;Blackout&#34; bar was a favorite watering hole for Coleman employees?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8292-->
<!--Post: 8293--><!--Time: 1109039709-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8293"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 21, 2005 - 6:35 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Whatthehell: First, thank you for your insight! That is the most well aticulated,thought provoking premise I&#39;ve read on this board in a while. I appreciate your efforts! As for Coleman, according to family and others, the reason they all worked at Coleman for so many years was simply;job security. When they all worked at Boeing or Cessna it seemed it resulted in a layoff, hard times etc. The Coleman Co. did provide an avenue of employment for machinist that were non-union but had a good pension, benefits, and job security. There were two plants one on Second &amp; St. Francis and the other, 37th St. North and Hydraulic. The Second &amp; St. Francis plant was primarily the corporate office which closed in the 80&#39;s, and the other remains. As for statistics, I&#39;m clueless. Maybe somewhere in the companies history page! I do remember the Williams, and I think at one time there was a Coleman bowling league that they all bowled together on. That would be the only connection that I could think of, or maybe some type of club with everyone on that line. Or drugs, which is certainly a possiblity. I worked at the corporate office downtown in the early 80&#39;s. I do remember when I worked there that several new computer programs were being designed for the plant&#40;I was dating one of the computer programmers, brought in from an outside firm&#41;And yes, he and his boss were freaks. I remember this guy telling me of some freakish stuff that went down in Texas before he came to Wichita. I don&#39;t remember the name of the programming firm, I do remember they were from Texas and I just now remembered his name. They of course didn&#39;t work on any assembly line, but they were in contact with plant personell. I too believe that there is a Coleman <BR>connection!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8293-->
